{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"unamused-caststeel-hapuka","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe S3 ticket-store backend (parent epic) relies on the `awslabs/git-remote-s3` remote\nhelper, which must be an **optional** dependency: core rebar stays lean (only pyyaml,\njsonschema, referencing are hard deps — see `pyproject.toml` `[project.optional-dependencies]`\nand `src/rebar/_optional.py`), and only operators using S3 install it. The version matters:\nthe multiple-bundles corruption the epic must prevent is *created* by a client without the\n`IfNoneMatch` per-ref lock; a live experiment this session verified `git-remote-s3` **0.3.2**\ncarries that lock (its `acquire_lock` uses `IfNoneMatch=\"*\"` with a TTL steal). So we need a\ndeclared extra AND a runtime probe that other children can call to prove the helper is\npresent and new enough.\n\nThis story delivers only the **dependency plumbing and a pure availability guard** — no\nbehavior is wired into init/push here (the preflight-enforcement child does that).\n\n## Approach\n\nFollow the existing optional-extra pattern exactly:\n\n1. In `pyproject.toml` `[project.optional-dependencies]`, add `s3 = [\"git-remote-s3>=0.3.2\"]`\n   with a comment (mirroring the `mcp`/`agents` entries) noting it ships the remote helper\n   and is never required by core rebar.\n2. In `src/rebar/_optional.py`, add an `EXTRAS` entry `\"s3\": (\"git_remote_s3\", \"<blurb>\")`\n   so a missing install produces the standard one-line actionable `pip install '.[s3]'`\n   message via the existing `guard_import` chokepoint.\n3. Add `require_s3_helper()` to `src/rebar/_optional.py`: it (a) confirms the `git-remote-s3`\n   console-script is on `PATH` (the helper is invoked by git as `git-remote-s3`, per the\n   epic's experiment) via `shutil.which`, and (b) checks the installed distribution version\n   with `importlib.metadata.version(\"git-remote-s3\")` against a module-level\n   `MIN_GIT_REMOTE_S3 = \"0.3.2\"` using `packaging`-free tuple comparison (stdlib only, to\n   keep `_optional.py` importable in the leanest install — the module's stated rule). It\n   returns a small result/raises a clear error naming the minimum. No import of the helper\n   package itself (it is a CLI, not a library).\n\nRationale: reusing `_optional.py`'s single-chokepoint pattern keeps the \"missing extra\"\nexperience consistent with every other rebar extra and keeps `import rebar` dependency-free,\nwhile a standalone `require_s3_helper()` gives the preflight and doctor children one tested\nseam to call rather than each re-implementing detection.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `pyproject.toml` — add the `s3` optional-dependency entry.\n- `src/rebar/_optional.py` — add the `s3` `EXTRAS` probe, `MIN_GIT_REMOTE_S3`, and\n  `require_s3_helper()`.\n- `tests/` — a new unit test module for `require_s3_helper()`.\n\nNo changes to store, config schema, or CLI behavior in this story.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: **pure-function / validation unit tests** (no git, no network — the guard is a\nPATH+version check). Oracles:\n- Happy: with `shutil.which` monkeypatched to a path and `importlib.metadata.version`\n  returning `\"0.3.2\"`, `require_s3_helper()` returns success.\n- Failure (missing): `shutil.which` returns `None` → raises the guard error naming\n  `pip install '.[s3]'` (assert the exact install string).\n- Edge (too old): version `\"0.2.9\"` → raises an error naming the `>=0.3.2` minimum.\n- Edge (newer): version `\"0.4.0\"` → success (lower-bound-only comparison).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `pyproject.toml` declares `s3 = [\"git-remote-s3>=0.3.2\"]` under\n      `[project.optional-dependencies]`; `pip install '.[s3]'` resolves the helper. Verified\n      by a build/metadata check that the extra is listed.\n- [ ] `src/rebar/_optional.py` exposes `require_s3_helper()` and a `\"s3\"` `EXTRAS` probe;\n      calling `guard_import(\"s3\")` when uninstalled yields the standard one-line message\n      naming `pip install '.[s3]'`. Verified by the unit test above.\n- [ ] `require_s3_helper()` returns success for a present helper at `>=0.3.2` and raises an\n      actionable error (naming the `0.3.2` minimum) when the helper is absent or older.\n      Verified by the four unit-test oracles above.\n- [ ] `_optional.py` remains stdlib-only (no import of `git_remote_s3` or third-party\n      version libs). Verified by inspection/the module import succeeding in a lean install.","id":"2b05-fcb5-7e87-41d9","parent_id":"cf3c-1444-863e-49d7","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Declare the optional [s3] extra and a git-remote-s3 availability/version guard"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786149273051529001,"uuid":"ac02bef0-9139-4a99-846e-f130b9c297df"}